course: biology
lesson: 26
title: Whales
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Whales are mammals, not fish: they breathe air, are warm-blooded, give birth to live babies and feed them milk, and their ancestors were land mammals that went back into the sea around 50 million years ago, so their closest living land relatives are hippos.
explain: Stand under the life-size blue whale model hanging in a museum: about 30 metres long, about as long as two and a half buses, around 150 tonnes, with one heart weighing about 180 kilograms, the biggest animal ever known.
explain: A whale breathes through a blowhole on top of its head, and the tall spout is warm wet breath turning to mist, the way your breath steams on a frosty morning.
explain: Cartoon whales shoot a fountain of sea water out of their heads and swallow ships whole; the spout is breath, not a fountain, and the biggest whale of all eats tiny krill only a few centimetres long.
explain: Its tail flukes beat up and down, not side to side like a fish, because the backbones of its land ancestors bent up and down when they ran.
fact: The blue whale is the largest animal ever known, around 30 metres long and about 150 tonnes or more, with a heart of about 180 kilograms; the water holds up its huge body, and on land its own weight would crush it.
fact: Whales are mammals: they breathe air, are warm-blooded, give birth to live young and feed them milk.
fact: Whales breathe through a blowhole on top of the head, and the spout is warm wet breath forming a cloud of mist.
fact: Baleen whales like humpbacks and blue whales have no teeth, but baleen plates that sieve krill and small fish from huge mouthfuls of water; a blue whale's throat is far too narrow to swallow a person.
fact: Humpback whales work together to blow a spiral net of bubbles that traps a shoal of fish, then rise through it with mouths open.
fact: Male humpbacks sing long songs that can last for hours; the males in one ocean area sing nearly the same song, and it slowly changes year by year.
fact: Sperm whales dive more than 1,000 metres deep, can hold their breath for over an hour, hunt squid using clicks, and have the biggest brain of any animal, around 8 kilograms.
fact: Breaching means leaping out of the water; scientists think whales do it to signal, show off or knock off skin pests.
fact: Whale tails move up and down, while fish tails move side to side, and humpbacks can be recognised by the pattern under their flukes.
fact: A whale calf is born tail first under water, so that it does not drown during the birth, and its mother helps it up for its first breath; a newborn blue whale can gain about 90 kilograms a day.
fact: Orcas, or killer whales, are the biggest kind of dolphin and live in family groups led by older females, who can live to around 80 or 90 and remember where to find food in hard times.
fact: Whaling, the hunting of whales for oil and meat, nearly wiped out some species; most whaling was banned in 1986, and humpbacks have recovered a lot.
fact: Whale poo spreads nutrients that feed plankton, and plankton makes much of Earth's oxygen.
exercise: About how long can a blue whale grow? | About 30 metres | About 3 metres | About 300 metres | About 10 metres
exercise: What is a whale's spout made of? | Warm wet breath turning to mist | Sea water it has swallowed | Steam from its hot boiling stomach | Bubbles from its gills
exercise: Where is a whale's blowhole? | On top of its head | At the end of its tail | Under its chin | On its belly
exercise: How does a humpback whale catch tiny fish without teeth? | It gulps water and sieves out the fish with baleen plates | It sucks them in through its blowhole | It stings them with its flippers | It waits with its mouth open for them to swim straight into its stomach
exercise: What is a humpback bubble net for? | Trapping a shoal of fish so the whales can gulp it | Keeping the whale calves warm | Helping the whales float on the surface while they are sleeping | Washing their skin clean of barnacles
exercise: Who sings the long humpback whale songs? | The males | The calves | The females only | Only whales that are lost
exercise: What is strange about humpback song in one ocean area? | All the males sing nearly the same song, and it changes year by year | Each whale sings one note its whole life | The song is exactly the same for a thousand years and never changes at all | Only one whale in each ocean can sing
exercise: How deep can a sperm whale dive? | More than 1,000 metres | About 10 metres | About 100 metres | About 50,000 metres
exercise: What does a sperm whale hunt in the dark deep sea? | Squid, found by clicks | Seals, found by smell | Krill, found by sight | Seaweed, found by touch
exercise: What do scientists think breaching might be for? | Signalling, showing off or knocking off skin pests | Catching birds flying overhead | Drying its skin in the sun | Getting rid of all the extra sea water it drank
exercise: Which statement shows that whales are mammals? | They breathe air and feed their babies milk | They have fins and live in water | They lay eggs on the seabed | They breathe through gills, just like sharks and other fish do
exercise: How does a whale's tail move when it swims? | Up and down | Side to side | Round in circles | It doesn't move, only the flippers do
exercise: How do scientists tell individual humpback whales apart? | By the black and white pattern under the tail flukes | By the length and the loudness of their song | By counting their teeth | By the colour of their spout
exercise: How is a whale calf born? | Tail first, under water | Head first, on a beach | From an egg laid on the seabed | Tail first, in a nest of seaweed
exercise: What is an orca really? | The biggest kind of dolphin | A small, black and white kind of shark | A kind of seal | A blue whale calf
exercise: Why does an aquarium sign say some whales almost disappeared? | People hunted them in huge numbers for oil and meat | They ate all the krill in the sea | Sharks hunted them out | They all swam to one ocean
test: in the cartoon the whale shoots a fountain of seawater out its head. true or nah? | Nah, the spout is its warm breath turning to mist | True, it squirts out all the sea water that it swallowed | True, but only after it eats a ship | Nah, whales have no blowhole
test: My little brother says whales are big fish cos they live in the sea. What's the best answer? | No, they breathe air and feed babies milk, so they are mammals | Yes, anything in the sea is a fish | Yes, but only the small ones | No, they are a kind of shark
test: Count them: a pod of four orcas swims past. How many hearts and how many tails? | 4 hearts and 4 tails | 8 hearts and 4 tails | 4 hearts and 8 tails | 16 hearts and 4 tails
test: Why could a blue whale never have lived on land at its size? | Water supports its weight, and on land its body would crush itself | Land is too warm for blue whales | It would get lost on land without the ocean currents to guide it | Its baleen would grow too long
test: What would happen to a whale calf if it were born head first and the birth took a long time? | It might drown before it could breathe | It would breathe through its skin | It would swim away at once | Nothing, whale calves can breathe under water for days
test: In the Pinocchio story a whale swallows a man whole. Why is this very unlikely for a blue whale? | Its throat is far too narrow, as it eats tiny krill | It only eats on Sundays | Its teeth would chew him up first | It lives far too deep in the sea for people to reach
test: Why do you think scientists study the up-and-down beat of a whale's tail as a clue to its past? | It is how its land-running ancestors' backbones bent | Whales copied it from fish | It is the only way to swim at all | It shows how deep the whale can dive
test: ugh whale poo, gross, why'd anyone care about that | It feeds plankton, and plankton makes much of Earth's oxygen | It is used to make whale oil | It keeps sharks away from beaches | It turns into the soft white sand that we find on the beach
test: Which animal is a whale's closest living land relative? | The hippo | The horse | The elephant | The walrus
test: Why might an old orca grandmother be so useful to her family? | She remembers where to find food in hard times | She is the fastest swimmer | She is the only one who can sing | She keeps all the young calves warm with her thick blubber
test: Most whaling was banned in 1986. What happened to humpback whales afterwards? | Their numbers recovered a lot | They died out completely | They turned into a new species | Nothing changed at all
test: A sperm whale dives for an hour. Which must it do before diving and after coming up? | Breathe air at the surface | Fill its gills with water | Eat a mouthful of plankton | Sleep on the surface for a day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the visible spout emitted by a surfacing whale? | It consists of exhaled warm and moist air forming a mist | It consists of seawater ingested and subsequently expelled | It consists of steam produced by digestion | It consists of gas expelled from the gills
exam: Identify the principal grounds upon which whales are classified as mammals rather than fish. | They respire air, are warm-blooded and suckle their young | They possess fins and inhabit the sea | They deposit eggs upon the seabed | They respire by means of gills
exam: Ascertain the direction in which the tail flukes of a whale are moved in swimming. | Vertically, that is, up and down | Laterally, that is, side to side | In a circular motion | They are held motionless
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dietary habit of the blue whale. | It subsists principally upon krill a few centimetres in length | It subsists principally upon large sharks | It subsists principally upon seals | It subsists principally upon seaweed gathered from the shallows of the coast
